Artemis the Ranger (wcristalle@hotmail.com) wrote:
: A little while ago in my current game I decided that I was ready to go
: after a mind flayer, which I had never dared to tackle close up
: before. Big mistake. My intelligence was down at about 4 before I
: zapped it with a wand of death.
: My unihorn took care of the intelligence drain quickly enough, but to
: my dismay I'd lost about half my inventory identities (and my level
: maps too, but I didn't know that at the time.) Fortunately, I had a
: fairly good idea of what I had where in my inventory and BoH, and I
: was able to reconstruct most of my identities in fairly short order.
: Except for my wands, that it. I had been carrying around about 20
: wands, most of different types, and had forgotten about 3/4 of their
: identities. The only two I remembered were the wand of death, because
: I remembered very clearly the number of charges on it, and my wand of
: cancellation, because...I had labelled it, "DON'T PUT IN BAG OF
: HOLDING!!!"

: My question is, is this method of dealing with the results of mind
: flayer attacks cheating? What if I had labelled ALL my wands prior to
: going after the mind flayer? I think the basic premise is that you're
: not supposed to know anything your character doesn't know, so it's a
: no-no to write down maps or object identities. But if your character
: gives an individual wand a particular name, perhaps they could
: remember this even if their knowledge of the wand's identity vanishes.
: (Something like, "Oh yeah, I had a wand of death over here in my back
: pocket. I don't remember anything about what it looked like,
: though...")

I don't think it is cheating.  I kind of think naming classes of objects 
is like your character writing on a notepad, "Crystal wand is wand of 
speed," after watching bugs speed up on the floor.  After ID-ing the wand, 
then meeting an unfriendly mind flayer and forgetting what the wand is, 
you can always look it up in your notepad.

